    "guide": "Detection and Incident Response work is the operational front line of a security team. Analysts watch alerts coming out of a SIEM or EDR platform, triage what looks real, and either close it out or escalate it into a formal incident. When something is confirmed, the same team runs containment, eviction, and recovery, then writes up what happened afterward. This is different from Threat Intelligence and Malware Research, which spends its time studying attacker groups and reverse engineering samples before an alert ever fires, and from Security Engineering, which builds and hardens the infrastructure that detection teams later monitor.\n\nIn the postings we track, titles carrying detection and response, SOC, incident response, or threat hunting all land in this category, and several companies use one job posting to cover more than one of those functions at once. Notion, Writer, and OpenAI post Security Engineer, Detection and Response roles that blend detection engineering with hands-on incident handling rather than splitting the two apart. Cloudflare and GitLab keep a dedicated incident response team, Cloudflare calls its program REACT and GitLab calls its team SIRT, separate from the engineers who build detections. Readers coming from a general security engineering background should expect these roles to weigh heavier on live triage and less on writing new infrastructure from scratch.\n\nOur tracked postings do not carry a structured certifications field, so we can't cite specific credential requirements straight from the data itself. One clearance driven listing, Woolly Bugger Solutions in Colorado Springs, lists Security Clearance as its only named requirement, which is typical of the government and defense contractor postings mixed into this category, including MANTECH, Leidos, Booz Allen Hamilton, and federal SOC roles at HRSA and the Supreme Court. Beyond that specific case, our data does not tell us which certifications recruiters are actually asking for, so we are not going to guess at names like GCIH or Security Plus here. Degree requirements are not captured in our fields either, and the seniority spread below suggests hands on triage experience carries more weight than any single credential in the roles we see posted.\n\nSeniority in this sample runs from a single internship, Schonfeld's 2027 Cybersecurity Operations Intern, and one junior forensic associate role, up through director level incident response leadership at CSL. Mid-level titles make up the largest single group, covering the bulk of the SOC analyst, detection engineer, and incident responder postings from companies like Notion, Cohere, and hellofresh. Senior, staff, and lead titles are common too, especially at AI infrastructure companies such as OpenAI, SentinelOne, Nebius, and CoreWeave that are building out dedicated detection and response functions from scratch. Junior hires appear to enter through SOC analyst or intern tracks, then move into detection engineering or incident command as they build up incident volume and hands on tooling experience.\n\nThe named tools we see most across this category are SIEM platforms and EDR tooling, both listed generically rather than by specific vendor name in our data. Cloud platform skills show up constantly, AWS most often, with Azure and GCP close behind, reflecting how much detection work now happens against cloud logs rather than on premise network taps. Python and Bash appear repeatedly as the scripting layer behind automation and triage tooling, and NIST framework knowledge is tagged often enough to suggest employers expect familiarity with a formal incident response standard. Threat hunting and detection engineering are tagged as distinct skills from plain incident response, which tracks with how the AI companies in this sample, OpenAI, Notion, Nebius, and Writer among them, are naming these roles.\n\nWe currently track approximately 57 open postings in Detection and Incident Response as of today. Only 14 of the 104 postings in our broader pull carry actual salary figures, and among those the range runs from 73,530 dollars to 225,000 dollars a year, pulled down at the low end by a single internship listing and up at the high end by a senior Booz Allen Hamilton SOC analyst role. The companies posting most often in this category are SentinelOne, OpenAI, ServiceNow, Writer, Nebius, and CoreWeave, alongside a steady stream of government and defense contractor listings from MANTECH, Leidos, and Booz Allen Hamilton. We also want to flag that our pull picked up a cluster of Tenstorrent semiconductor chip design postings that use SoC in the System on Chip hardware sense rather than Security Operations Center, which is a labeling overlap in the source data rather than a real signal about this discipline.",
